This article presents a set of templates for the \TeX 4ht tool, which is used to convert 
\LaTeX\ documents into \acro{HTML}. These templates facilitate the publication of various 
types of documents on the web and provide processing and automation capabilities. 

The first template is intended for converting book-style documents into a web format. 
It allows splitting the text into individual chapters with automatically generated 
navigation and support for responsive design, so the result is readable even on 
mobile devices. The second template serves for creating statically generated blogs. Each post is 
written as a separate \LaTeX\ document, which is converted to \acro{HTML} using \TeX 4ht. 
Subsequently, these articles are processed by a static site generator, such as Jekyll, 
which handles the assembly of the entire blog, creation of indexes, archives, and other navigation. 
The third template is focused on converting presentations created in the Beamer environment 
into so-called handouts\Dash overview materials for the audience. The result is a 
readable and well-structured web document suitable for sharing after a lecture. 

All templates are designed to work with GitHub Actions. This 
means that the documents can be automatically compiled and published online 
whenever a change is made in the repository.
